<h2>An unusually well-diversified trade</h2>
<p>Most of the product lines we've researched show heavy concentration in one or two markets — China for chilli and cumin, Indonesia for groundnut, Bangladesh for lentils. Chickpeas break that pattern. The UAE leads at USD 35.3 million, closely followed by Bangladesh (USD 34.2 million), Iran (USD 28.3 million), Algeria (USD 26.0 million) and Sri Lanka (USD 24.1 million) — no single market above roughly 12.5% of India's total USD 283.2 million in 2024 chickpea exports. If you're evaluating supply-chain risk, this is one of the more resilient product categories in terms of demand-side diversification.</p>

<h2>Kabuli vs. desi: know which you need</h2>
<p>Kabuli chickpeas are the larger, lighter-coloured, thin-skinned type used whole — familiar as the base for hummus and similar preparations — and graded by count per ounce, where a lower count number means larger chickpeas and a higher price. Desi chickpeas are smaller, darker and thicker-skinned, and are frequently split into chana dal for South Asian culinary use. These are genuinely different products commercially, not just cosmetic variants, so specify which you need rather than ordering "chickpeas" generically.</p>

<h2>Specification for a UAE order</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Type</strong> — kabuli or desi, whole or split</li>
  <li><strong>Count</strong> (kabuli) — 42-44, 58-60 or your target grade</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture</strong> — typically ≤12%</li>
  <li><strong>Admixture and defect tolerance</strong> — damaged, discoloured or split percentage</li>
  <li><strong>Purity</strong> — the percentage of the stated type in the lot</li>
</ul>

<h2>Documentation and storage</h2>
<p>Chickpeas are a stored product, so fumigation is frequently required to protect against insect infestation during transit and storage — we arrange this and supply the certificate as standard. Phytosanitary certificate and certificate of origin round out the typical documentation set. See our <a href="/markets/uae/">UAE market guide</a> for ports, transit times and broader import requirements.</p>

<h2>Export policy: worth a brief note</h2>
<p>Unlike onion, non-basmati rice or wheat, chickpeas haven't faced the same history of Indian export restrictions — and the strong, sustained 2024 export volumes are themselves evidence the category was freely exportable throughout the period. That said, we confirm current DGFT status at the time of every order regardless of product, as standard practice, rather than assuming policy is fixed.</p>

<h2>A note on India's own pulse import position</h2>
<p>Worth knowing as background: India is not purely a chickpea surplus producer feeding export demand from excess supply. Domestic consumption of pulses generally is enormous, and India has at various points run duty-free import windows for certain pulses to manage domestic supply gaps. This doesn't affect chickpea export availability specifically in the way it does for a product like masoor lentils, but it's a useful piece of context for understanding how Indian pulse trade policy works more broadly — export and import measures on different pulse types can move independently of each other based on that specific crop's domestic supply position in a given year.</p>
